-## Three numbers that must not share a denominator
+## Keep colour strength, carotenoid composition and pungency separate
 
-The JECFA specification lists total carotenoids at not less than 7%, while capsanthin is not less than 30% **of total carotenoids**.[3] These are reference-specification statements, not ZL specifications, current stock values or certificates of analysis. The second percentage is not 30% capsanthin in the supplied preparation.
+The JECFA specification lists total carotenoids at not less than 7%, while capsanthin is not less than 30% **of total carotenoids**.[2] These are reference-specification statements, not ZL specifications, current stock values or certificates of analysis. The second percentage is not 30% capsanthin in the supplied preparation. These thresholds describe the extract in the cited reference specification; do not apply them automatically to a diluted or formulated preparation without identifying the material and reporting basis.
 
-The source also assigns different analytical procedures: spectrophotometry for total carotenoids and HPLC for capsanthin.[3] Ask a supplier to identify the analyte, method, unit and reporting basis for every quoted value. Do not silently substitute a colour-strength number for a carotenoid mass percentage, or a pigment-composition percentage for the strength of the full commercial preparation.
+The source also assigns different analytical procedures: spectrophotometry for total carotenoids and HPLC for capsanthin.[2] Ask a supplier to identify the analyte, method, unit and reporting basis for every quoted value. Do not silently substitute a colour-strength number for a carotenoid mass percentage, or a pigment-composition percentage for the strength of the full commercial preparation.
 
-Finally, retain capsaicinoids as a separate line. The reference specification gives them a separate limit and test.[3] A redder sample is not an analytical report of pungency. If a supplier offers a “de-flavoured” preparation, request the process description and sample for sensory evaluation: the technical assessment describes further processing to reduce flavour and aroma, but this does not establish that every offered grade is flavour-free.[2]
